Performance Cabinet Panel","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.hertfordshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-08","committee":"Highways Cabinet Panel","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.hertfordshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-09","committee":"Hertfordshire Fire Pension Board","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.hertfordshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-14","committee":"Cabinet","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.hertfordshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-15","committee":"Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.hertfordshire.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-14","committee":"Public Health & Community Safety Cabinet Panel","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.hertfordshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Health Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed; Patient Experience","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.hertfordshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.hertfordshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-18","committee":"Pensions Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.hertfordshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-23","committee":"Border to Coast Pension Partnership Ltd investment pool Joint Governance Committee","time":"11.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.hertfordshire.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"PWLB borrowing rates ranged from 4.59% to 5.48% over the year; average 4.84%","lead":"The council executed most of its refinancing and new borrowing during a volatile rate environment. The average rate of 4.84% is materially higher than rates available in prior years. Any delay in refinancing existing debt exposes the council to the risk of\u2026","iso":"2026-07-24","paper_title":"02. Treasury management year end report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://hertfordshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s77367/77367.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Pooled fund investments carry \u00a31.4m unrealised loss; market value \u00a327.6m on \u00a329m originally invested","lead":"The council is holding investments at a loss while carrying net borrowing of \u00a3649.4m. There is an implicit signal that the council may liquidate these funds if market conditions allow, which could affect cash flow projections. The statutory override\u2026","iso":"2026-07-24","paper_title":"02. Treasury management year end report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://hertfordshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s77367/77367.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"New long-term borrowing of \u00a3215m undertaken in year; includes \u00a3130.6m for capital programme and \u00a384.4m for debt refinancing","lead":"Total borrowing of \u00a3215m is material and shows the council's sustained capital financing need. The split between funding new investment (\u00a3130.6m) and refinancing (\u00a384.4m) is important for understanding whether the council is growing its debt stock or\u2026","iso":"2026-07-24","paper_title":"02. Treasury management year end report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://hertfordshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s77367/77367.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-07-24","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"02. Treasury management year end report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://hertfordshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s77367/77367.pdf","headline":"PWLB borrowing rates ranged from 4.59% to 5.48% over the year; average 4.84%","detail":"Long-term borrowing rates offered by the PWLB showed significant volatility during 2025/26, with 10-year rates ranging from a low of 4.59% to a high of 5.48%. The average rate for the period was 4.84%. The report notes that the financial year was dominated by two periods of significant uncertainty: US trade tariffs in April 2025 and the US/Israel war with Iran in February 2026, both of which drove market volatility.","category":"borrowing","matters":"The council executed most of its refinancing and new borrowing during a volatile rate environment. The average rate of 4.84% is materially higher than rates available in prior years. Any delay in refinancing existing debt exposes the council to the risk of locking in these elevated rates. The geopolitical and trade uncertainty noted is ongoing.","figures":"10-year PWLB rates: low 4.59%, high 5.48%, average 4.84%","quote":"As shown in the graph in section 5.11, the rates for long term borrowing continue to be volatile, with the lowest rate on a PWLB 10-year EIP loan in the period being 4.59% and the highest being 5.48%. The average rate for the period was 4.84%.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-24","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"02. Treasury management year end report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://hertfordshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s77367/77367.pdf","headline":"Pooled fund investments carry \u00a31.4m unrealised loss; market value \u00a327.6m on \u00a329m originally invested","detail":"The council holds \u00a329m in strategic pooled funds (property, bond, equity, multi-asset) with a current market value of \u00a327.6m, representing an unrealised loss of \u00a31.4m. This loss is absorbed by a separate unusable reserve under a statutory override that continues until 1 April 2029 for legacy investments. The council notes that as a net borrower, the holding of these long-term investments will be kept under review, and a decision may be taken to exit when viable.","category":"reserves","matters":"The council is holding investments at a loss while carrying net borrowing of \u00a3649.4m. There is an implicit signal that the council may liquidate these funds if market conditions allow, which could affect cash flow projections. The statutory override provides breathing room until 2029, but the override ends on 1 April 2028 for non-legacy investments.","figures":"\u00a329m invested; \u00a327.6m market value; \u00a31.4m unrealised loss; statutory override continues until 1 April 2029","quote":"As at 31 March 2026, the County Council held \u00a329.0m in a number of diversified pooled funds. The market value of these investments was \u00a327.6m, representing a capital loss of \u00a31.4m on the amount originally invested. However, given that the County Council is a net borrower, the holding of these long-term investments will be kept under review and a decision may be taken to exit from these funds when viable to do so.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-24","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"02. Treasury management year end report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://hertfordshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s77367/77367.pdf","headline":"New long-term borrowing of \u00a3215m undertaken in year; includes \u00a3130.6m for capital programme and \u00a384.4m for debt refinancing","detail":"The council undertook significant new borrowing of \u00a3215m during 2025/26. This comprised \u00a3130.6m to fund the capital programme and \u00a384.4m to refinance external debt (including the LOBO repayment and commercial debt prepayment noted above). The council's stated strategy is gradual borrowing to avoid having to borrow significantly when rates spike.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Total borrowing of \u00a3215m is material and shows the council's sustained capital financing need. The split between funding new investment (\u00a3130.6m) and refinancing (\u00a384.4m) is important for understanding whether the council is growing its debt stock or managing the maturity profile. The gradual approach is prudent, but rates have been volatile.","figures":"\u00a3215m new long-term borrowing; \u00a3130.6m for capital programme; \u00a384.4m for debt refinancing","quote":"During the year to 31 March 2026, new long-term borrowing of \u00a3215m was undertaken, this included \u00a3130.6m to fund the capital programme plus \u00a384.4m to refinance external debt (as set out in sections 8.10 and 8.13).","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-24","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"02. Treasury management year end report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://hertfordshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s77367/77367.pdf","headline":"LOBO loan of \u00a39.4m called for refinancing; council repaid and refinanced through PWLB","detail":"In June 2025, the council was notified of an intended rate increase on a LOBO (Lender's Option Borrower's Option) loan. The council exercised its option to repay the \u00a39.4m loan at no additional cost and refinanced it through the PWLB. LOBO loans carry inherent refinancing risk because the lender can force a rate increase at set dates.","category":"borrowing","matters":"This shows active management of refinancing risk but also demonstrates that the council was exposed to forced refinancing at an inopportune time. The council removed \u00a39.4m of LOBO exposure, which is favourable, but signals that commercial lenders were tightening terms.","figures":"\u00a39.4m LOBO loan repaid; refinanced through PWLB","quote":"At the start of the financial year, the Authority held \u00a319.4m of LOBO's (Lender's Option Borrower's Option) loans where the lender has the option to propose an increase in the interest rate at set dates, following which the County Council has the option to either accept the new rate or to repay the loan at no additional cost. In June 2025, the County Council was notified of an intended increase in the rate on one of its outstanding LOBO loans and consequently took the decision to repay the \u00a39.4m loan and has subsequently refinanced this through a loan from the PWLB.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-24","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"02. Treasury management year end report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://hertfordshire.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s77367/77367.pdf","headline":"\u00a391m commercial debt prepaid at discount; new PWLB loans at higher rates (5.35%) but offset by reduced principal; annual revenue benefit \u00a31.6m","detail":"In July 2025, the council prepaid \u00a391m of commercial bank loans carrying an average rate of 4.46%, achieving a redemption cost of \u00a375m. This negotiated discount of \u00a316m (17.6% reduction) will be amortised over 10 years, yielding annual revenue benefit of \u00a31.6m. The redeemed loans were refinanced with PWLB medium-term loans at higher rates (5.35%), but the council benefits from paying interest on a reduced principal amount.","category":"borrowing","matters":"The council took advantage of a window to lock in debt savings. However, it has shifted to higher nominal rates (5.35% vs 4.46%), creating exposure if rates fall further. The \u00a31.6m annual benefit helps offset future budget pressures but represents a timing-dependent strategic choice.","figures":"\u00a391m commercial loans repaid; redemption cost \u00a375m; discount \u00a316m; replacement rate 5.35%; annual benefit \u00a31.6m amortised over 10 years","quote":"In July 2025, the County Council prepaid \u00a391m of loans from commercial banks running at an average rate of 4.46%, at a redemption cost of \u00a375m. In line with capital finance regulations, the discount of \u00a316m (the difference between the loan principal and redemption cost) will be amortised over 10 years, providing an annual revenue benefit of \u00a31.6m for the Council. The redeemed loans have been refinanced by medium-term loans from the PWLB. While the new loans are at higher interest rates (5.35%), this will be offset by savings generated by paying interest on a reduced loan principal.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-07-24"},"name":"Hertfordshire CC","findings_total":73,"news_drafts_total":3}}